**Definition 5.6 (realized-body validation and data-dependence restriction).**
On a supplied view body derive a candidate historical prefix using only
that body, its identity binding and the fixed public code/layout:
S or B first gives raw $Y$, selected M and extracted data; M gives those
data directly; T gives its checked erasure/execution. Replay where needed,
check seed validity at the original entrance, exact $k$, target source/origin
binding, target activation when present and target phase, then re-encode that candidate's
same view and require exact body equality. Binding checks follow actual
referenced origin chains in this reconstructed description and compare
their target fields to the selected completed-object record. Check its
later-service fields separately as Definition 3.5 specifies. They are not a new live
producer query. Full malformed-word authenticity is not inferred from
self-consistency; the selected exclusive-writer/sealed-copy route supplies
actual provenance.

In a direct view branch the logical inputs to all these helpers are
$(v,b,\mathsf{PublicCode})$ only. PublicCode contains original public
$H,\xi$, descriptor/layout conventions, $D_0,L$, captured $\Delta(D_2)$
and the corresponding fixed boot/compiler conventions. It excludes
manifest seed contents, old-frame words and package reservoirs. The code
may derive those data from $v$ when the view supplies them. The handler
does not access `images`, image payloads or a package's replay reservoir
to repair a failed direct decoder. Failure publishes InvalidView.

Physical bank rebuilding still copies every persistent field, including
the old service manifest. That incidental compiler copy is counted and
retained. It is not a value used by a direct decoder: its word-level
dependency graph reads only the three displayed arguments and its own
temporaries. The old-image fallback exclusion is this explicit dependency
property, not a claim that earlier source bytes cease to exist.

